1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,680
In this video, we're going to show you how you can use CI-driven scans in Burp Suite Enterprise

2
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:15,360
when you're using GitLab as your CI provider. Now to do this, you'll have already needed to

3
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:20,480
have created a user in Burp with an API key which has permissions to run CI-driven scans.

4
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:24,720
If you need help with this, you can look at our documentation pages on our website.

5
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:32,640
Now in my GitLab repository here, I have four files, but the main ones are the GitLab CI file for the pipeline,

6
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:37,840
the Burp config file for the scan configuration, and the recorded login JSON file.

7
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:42,880
Now our pipeline file just says that we're going to run our Docker container, and it points to our

8
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:47,760
configuration file for the scan, and it's also going to say to output a JUnit report file when the scan

9
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:52,720
finishes. And in this artifacts rule, we say that we're going to pull out this JUnit report file,

10
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:55,360
and then report those tests directly in the pipeline.

11
00:00:57,120 --> 00:01:01,680
If we go back and look at our Burp configuration file, we can see that it points to our Enterprise

12
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:07,120
server, and we also have our API key here for authentication. If you're using a self-signed

13
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:10,960
certificate as well, you'll need to include the public key here of that certificate to prove the

14
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:16,480
authenticity of the server. If you scroll down, we have a correlation ID, which is the name that the

15
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:20,880
site will show us in the Burp console. If you don't want the site to show in the Burp console,

16
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:25,120
then you don't need to have a correlation ID. We also have the start URLs, and all of this

17
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:32,320
configuration here is the same as what we have in the Burp console. Under the reporting section,

18
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:36,240
we say that we're going to fail the build if we have a low severity issue with a firm confidence.

19
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:40,400
But all the settings here are just a snapshot of what you can set for a CI-driven scan.

20
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:45,760
On our GitHub, we have a publicly accessible repository which shows all the different settings that you

21
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:49,520
can set within a CI-driven scan. You can find it at the link that's on the screen.

22
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:54,880
Now, if we go back to GitLab and kick off a new build,

23
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:59,760
if we go to pipelines, and then we run our pipeline.

24
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:05,680
You can see here that pipeline is now starting, and if we go to our jobs,

25
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:10,240
you can see here that it's found our Burp config path and run the test.

26
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:15,360
Now, if we go to check our Burp, under Scans, we can see that our new scan has been started.

27
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:20,000
And if we go to view the site, we can see here that the site was created by CI-driven scan.

28
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:23,840
If we go to a scan which was already completed in the past,

29
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:32,800
you can see here all the different statistics of the scan as it ran, and we can see at the end here

30
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:37,120
that the job failed with exit status 1 because we reached our issue threshold here.

31
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:41,680
So if we go back to the job, and then we go to the test pane,

32
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:46,800
you can see here all the tests that were run during the scan and the ones that failed.

33
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:52,080
And if we go to view details, we can see in here the severity, confidence, host, and path of the issue,

34
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:55,680
as well as the issue detail and background, and all of the same information that you're used to seeing

35
00:02:55,680 --> 00:03:00,160
within the Burp console. And that's how you run a CI-driven scan in Burp when using GitLab.

36
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:05,840
So, let's see.

37
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:09,840
So, let's see.

